ion in an identical  manner to females that have circulating  estrogen presumably have circulating  estrogen and progesterone there's no  difference in their in their viral  titers in their lungs but when we looked  at the estrus cycle of these animals  what we found was that being infected  with influenza significantly disrupts  the estrus cycle which for mice is the  reproductive cycle it's what we would  refer to as the menstrual cycle in mice  their estrus cycle was significantly  increased and this was predominantly  caused by an increase in the stage of  estrus called die Astra's which is when  both estrogen and progesterone are at  their nadir suggesting that influenza is  disrupting female reproductive cycle  making a go Natalie intact  female more similar to an a very  optimized female so what happens if we  give hormones that well one hormone that  we have given back is progesterone which  is notoriously known for being  anti-inflammatory and because we knew  that influenza infection disrupts the  reproductive cycle of females causing a  persistent state of die  estrous or causing a persistent state of  low progesterone and low estrogen we've  wondered can hormone replacement therapy  improve the outcome of influenza and  females we've done this with both  progesterone and estrogen but I'd like  to walk you through our story with  progesterone because those are some of  our most recent data so when  progesterone crosses the lipid bilayer  of a cell to bind to cells to bind to  its receptor located in lots of  different cell types ranging from  various epithelial cells to diverse  immune cells the binding of progesterone  to the progesterone receptor can  actually block the activity of  inflammatory proteins including NF  kappa-b  which is illustrated here in this  cartoon the progesterone progesterone  receptor complex can also translocate  the nucleus of a cell where it can bind  to a progesterone response element  located on a stretch of DNA where it can