eated with progesterone as well as  females not treated with progesterone we  find that treatment with progesterone  significantly increases the mRNA as well  as amber regulan protein production in  the lungs of females during influenza  infection we also find so again speaking  to this growth factor with more growth  factor you're going to get less damage  we're going to have greater repair of  lung tissue with increased repair of  lung tissue there's going to be less  protein being released into the bronco  alveolar spaces we can measure this by  looking at the amount of protein present  in Branko alveolar lavage  abbreviated ba L in panel a the amount  of protein contained in this lavage  fluid and what we find is that emails  that have been treated with progesterone  have less protein in their lungs as  compared in the bronco alveolar spaces  to be more specific as compared with  females treated with progesterone we  also find that proliferation of cells is  increased in the presence of  progesterone during influenza infection  which is abbreviated in piano B iae so  the way that we can measure cellular  proliferation is to look at the  expression of a marker referred to as  ki-67 and what we have in panel B is the  quantification of data from many animals  both mock infected as well as influenza  infected again abbreviated IAB and you  can see based on our cumulative data  females treated with infected females  treated with progesterone have  significantly greater proportions of  ki67 positive cells and in panel C we  just have the images representative  images to show you that among infected  females treated with progesterone you  have a greater degree of staining for  ki-67 which is illustrated by this dark  brown punctate staining which although  many cells have this staining we were  quite interested to see that you see  such a profound amount of staining in  the epithelial barrier again looking at  pulmonary function so we talked about  this earlier this lung diffusing  capacity or gas exchange that's exchange  of oxygen and co2 in the lungs and what  we found was that this measure of  pulmonary function with the stippled  lines illustrating the physiological  range for mock infected animals  it's females that have been treated with  progesterone that return back to  baseline pulmonary function faster than  females that have been  pleaded progesterone to further  illustrate mechanistically that  progesterone is having its reparative or  protective effects during influenza  infection through ampere regulan  signaling we want we studied and and or  excuse me we compared the responses to  progesterone in females that were  wild-type females so these are  genetically intact emails and compared  them with what are referred to as ampere  regulan knockout females so these are  females that do not have the ample  regulan gene within their genome when we  treat these females with progesterone  and infect them with influenza what we  find is that treatment progesterone  treatment of ampere regulan depleted  females does not protect them from flu  they experience greater morbidity as  compared with wild-type females and they  experience greater inflammation as  compared with wild-type emails so  deletion of ampere regulan reverses the  protective effects of progesterone I'd  last like to make the point that we can  use synthetic forms of progesterone  including lever nor gesture all so this  would be a form of progesterone that is  found in several types of birth control  if we give females lever no gesture all  which is abbreviated LNG very similar to  what we see with progesterone these  females are in fact protected against  influenza they experience less morbidity  as illustrated in panels a and B despite  having no difference in the ability to  control the viral infection which again  has suggested to us that it's about  modulating the inflammation as opposed  to the control of infection that  mediates the protective effects of these  progestin z' in email nice another  mechanism that can protect female  is giving them a vaccine so vaccinating  females against influence turns out to  be a wonderful way to improve protection  males and females differ in their  responses to diverse vaccine so while I  will be focused on influenza I do not  want you to think there is something  singularly unique about influenza there  are actually several vaccines that are  given to children adults aged adults  that when we look at the immune response  we find it is greater in emails as  compared with males  the downside of greater immune  activation could be that females are  also significantly more likely to report  adverse reactions to vaccines so where  investigators have bothered to look and  compare males and females statistically  they report adverse reactions being  higher or more likely to be reported in  females as compared with males but as  you can see especially among children  there are several studies that have not  partitioned and analyzed data by stats  so again turning our attention to  influenza these are data from a human  trial of the trivalent inactivated  influenza abbreviated TIV so this is the  inactivated flu vaccine that is given  intramuscularly often referred to as the  flu shot and what we can see is that  whether females and males are given a  full dose so that's the dose that we all  receive when we go to receive our annual  flu vaccine or whether females and males  receive a half dose of the trivalent  inactivated flu vaccine females develop  higher hemagglutinin inhibition antibody  titers as compared with males what I  find remarkable is that females given a  half dose of the vaccine mount an immune  response  that is almost equivalent to that of  males that receive the full dose when we  look at the percentage of Saxony's  reporting adverse side effects we find  that whether we look at injection site  pain inflammation muscle pain or  headache females are significantly more  likely to report these adverse reactions  as compared with males now whether this  is a gender difference illustrating a  reporting bias or whether females are in  fact experiencing greater inflammation  has not been completely determined but I  would argue that based on our data we  might hypothesize that inflammation  could be greater in females as compared  with males resulting in a greater  reporting of these adverse side effects  but we can recreate these differences in  animal models using both killed  influenza vaccine as well as live  influenza vaccine again the live  influenza vaccine is the vaccine that is  given intranasally often referred to as  FluMist which is currently no longer  being offered in the United States  though it is being offered in several  other countries including Canada but  what we find in our mouse studies is  that if we give males and females again  of reproductive ages either the killed  influenza vaccine or alive influenza  vaccine and we measure antibody  responses so if we look in the upper  panel in response to the kill vaccine we  can look at total influenza specific IgG  titers or we can look at neutralizing  antibody titers so this is a very  functional assay in which we are able to  actually take serum from animals we even  do this with human samples and expose it  to virus and cells that are virally  infected and we look at the ability of  this antibody to block the ability of  virus to infect cells very highly  functional assay and what we find is  females that have been vaccinated with  the kill vaccine developed higher  antibody responses ads compared with  nails using a live influenza vaccine we  find that females also produce higher  antibody titers so NAB is just a fancy  abbreviation for neutralizing antibody  titers when we look in the lungs of  females that have received a live  influenza vaccine we find that females  have more antibody secreting cells in  their lungs I've compared with males  when we also look in the lungs for those  cd8 positive T cells those T cells that  have very important antiviral properties  and we look at various populations of  memory cells and what I have pictured  here for you today are the t RM cells  which are just the resident memory T  cells these are very important in in the  memory response and env protection  against challenge from influenza what we  find are that these resident memory T  cells are found in a higher in a higher  degree to get greater numbers of these  cells in the lungs of vaccinated females  as compared with nails well one thing  that we can more readily do in mice than  we could in humans is we can actually  challenge them with live virus it's not  to say we can't do this in humans it's  just a more challenging IRB approval  protocol so in this case what we do is  we challenge our animals with a drift  variant of the 2009 h1n1 influenza  vaccine or excuse me virus and this  variant this drip variant has a mutation  in position 166 in the hemagglutinin  head and so in panel B here what this is  based on our data from humans in which  beginning in 2013  this mutation at position 166 of a k2  Accu occurred and has actually become  the predominant mutation that we now see  in circulating strains of h1n1 and  seasonal e when we inoculate vaccinated  males and females or challenged  vaccinated males and females with this  drift Arian we find that in the upper  panel females are able to clear virus  faster so again using that tcid 50  method to measure the amount of virus  and the lungs of males and females  females are clearing virus significantly  faster than males and when we look at  morbidity following infection females  are not actually experiencing any  morbidity and they're experiencing  significantly less morbidity as compared  with their vaccinated male counterparts  suggesting that vaccine efficacy may be  greater in females as compared with  nails the last point that I would like  to briefly make is that I do not think  this is limited to influenza more  recently we've been working with  immunization protocols against malaria  parasite and what we find is that  following vaccination with irradiated  malaria parasites and these are  irradiated sporozoites  females again adult females of  reproductive ages generate higher  antibody responses to the vaccine as  compared with their male counterparts  I'm showing you in the upper panel their  titration curves if we look at the area  under the curve you find that females  have higher antibody responses of  compared with nails looking at area  under the curve looking at total IgG  titers in the bottom left panel or  looking at the titer of antibody when  that antibody crosses the od1 so  regardless of how we slice these data we  find the females  hire anybody responses in this case to  an irradiated or azoic vaccine against  malaria when we challenged our male and  female mice with malaria parasites and  in this case we use the mosquito bite  challenge what we find is that females  that have been challenged with malaria  develop a significantly higher cd8  positive T cell response this time in  their liver because that is where the  malaria parasite goes females have  higher t-cell responses and when we  measure parasite burden in their liver  so looking at in this case Plasmodium  RNA copy numbers what we find is that  vaccinated females have virtually  undetectable levels of parasite RNA  whereas vaccinated males though they do  have less Peris less of a parasite load  than unvaccinated or naive males they  still have detectable parasites and a  level of parasite detection that is  significantly higher than their  vaccinated female counterparts so what I  hope that I have showed you today is  that there are several modifiers of sex  differences and infectious disease  outcome I've only discussed but one of  those modifiers sex hormones I've shown  you that testosterone can protect  against severe disease outcome in males  I've shown you that gesture own can  protect against severe disease outcome  in females and I've shown you that when  comparing males and females vaccination  may be one of the best ways to keep both  males and females within the range of an  immune response that leads to the least  amount of host damage and so with that I  would like to end and paint several of  my collaborators at Johns Hopkins at the  FDA the students who have contributed  significantly to the work that I've  presented as well as my funding sources  so thank you  Thank You dr. Klein for that  presentation it's time for QA if you  have a question you'd like to ask please  do so now just click on the green Q&amp;A  button at the lower left of the  presentation window type your question  into the box that appears on your screen  to click on the sidon button will answer  as many of your questions as we have  time for if we are unable to get to your  question we will follow up with you via  email after the presentation let's get  started our first question is Thank You  Enza and the other infectious diseases  are most severe in the elderly do you  see the same sex differences in older  aged aged individuals and what about  children that's a great question and the  comment that I made that these sex  differences are by no means stagnant  they do in fact change over our life  course so if we take influenza as but  one example we find that influence the  severity of influenza the severe in all  children and all older adults data show  that it is significantly more severe in  boys of compared with girls prior to  puberty as well as in older aged men as  compared with women in fact there are  even beautiful data that have come out  of various parts of Asia showing that in  community-dwelling  older individuals who have been  vaccinated you're significantly more  likely to see breakthrough cases of  influenza among vaccinated males as  compared with females suggesting that  the vaccine efficacy may be even worse  among older males as compared with  females  thank you for that answer we'll wrap up  with one more question and that question  is most vaccines are given to children  before puberty are the same sex  differences in vaccine efficacy observed  if so what is the mechanism mediating  these differences well that is a great  question and and a question that we  continue to give a lot of attention to  in my laboratory what we do in fact find  is that sex differences are reported in  children under the age of five in which  in particular antibody responses are  often reported to the greater following  vaccination of females or girls as  compared with boys because these are  children under the age of five they've  not yet gone through puberty they do not  have those circulating testosterone and  progesterone concentrations that I was  referring to earlier in my in my talk so  what we've begun thinking is that there  could either be genetic or maybe even  epigenetic differences between males and  females that could be mediating some of  these differences and that while I've  given you evidence that sex steroid  hormones can regulate the immune  response they are by no means the sole  mediators of these differences in our  immune responses or in the efficacy of  vaccines there's growing information or  growing data illustrating that not only  can there be genetic differences but  that differences between males and  females in the composition of our  microbiome can impact our immune systems  differently and result in differences  not only in vaccine efficacy but maybe  even in development of autoimmune  diseases which also show a sex bias with  over 80% of all autoimmune disease  patients being female so  there's still much to be learned I would  like to once again thank dr. Klein for  her presentation do you have any final  comments for including me in this lab  Reid's webinar I think that bringing  diverse science to the masses through  online learning is a very very valuable  tool and I am honored to have been here  and I hope that everybody has learned a  little bit more about the consideration  of sex as a biological variable and may  go back to their own labs or to their  own patients and think that there may be  some fundamental differences between men  and women which means we may have to be  treated differently in order to be  treated equally thank you so much thank  you once again  decline I would also like to thank lab  roots for making today's educational  webcast possible before we go I want to  let everyone know that today's webcast  will be available for on-demand viewing  through December 14th 2017 you will  receive an email from labrys letting you  know when this webcast will be available  to replay please share that announcement  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event that's all for now  thank you for joining us we hope to see  you again soon goodbye
 

